reened out I don't know what  that says about me am i normal to  inventor what kind of number of people  get screened out yeah well you're in  very good company goodness so about 99%  of men who apply to become sperm donors  will be screened out so sperm banks are  looking for men who are extremely  fertile with very high sperm counts so  if you look at a sort of bell curve of  fertility they're looking for men in the  two to three percent of sperm count  that's because they freeze the sperm or  cryopreserve it that kills off about  half the cells so that's right there  knocking out most men who apply and then  the men who make it through that test  need three generations of their family  health history to look pretty good so  men who have you know a lot of heart  attacks or mental illness or alcoholism  in their family won't make it through  the screening process and then they have  to be willing to go in at least once a  week for a year to make donations and so  you know if you have a hundred men who  make that first call to the sperm bank  you're really walking out the door with  about one percent of the people making  it through that process so there's a lot  of screening that happens and I know  you've written your work about how men  are paid so just because I donate sperm  does that mean I automatically get paid  for that donation no it doesn't so in  sperm banks men go in and they make this  donation if their sperm count is high  enough to meet the bank standards then  they will be paid for that donation but  all kinds of things can cause that sperm  count to drop including being too  stressed out say by your finals in  school if you're drinking too much  alcohol if you're not getting enough  rest if you're sick so all of these  things can cause sperm count to drop so  men's sperm count which you know another  thing that people don't realize is men  sperm count really fluctuates over days  and months and so sperm donors have to  stay pretty healthy and take 