estion about how men are recruited so  how are sperm donors recruited what's  the messaging about sperm donation tell  me a little bit more about that yeah  well historically sperm banks really  relied on this idea that sperm donation  was this easy thing it's something  that's a little bit funny you know they  make jokes in their advertisements and  so the advertisements really focus on  cartoon images of sperm there might be  some small print about helping somebody  to become a family but the focus is  really on you know come in and get paid  for something you're already doing to  quote one of the ads so they draw on  these sort of cheeky advertisements in  order to recruit men in because they're  dealing with the college-age population  for the most part but donation is not  really framed for men as something that  is important or you know really  contributing to the lives of others by  helping them have families the focus  really is on come in and you know  provide some sperm and get paid so let's  talk a little bit about the way in which  these men are recruited and in  particular sort of gendered messaging in  the croute meant and the conceptions of  employment and this is a really  important part of your work I know  really the main argument of my book is  that this language of donation suffuses  both the world of egg agencies and sperm  banks but in sperm banks a paved  donation is understood as a job they  think of men as employees they cut them  paychecks every two weeks they talk to  them about this is something they need  to show up for on a regular basis clock  in and clock out and produce a passable  sample  and an egg donation that paid donation  is really understood as a gift from one  woman to another and that it's in a gift  in the service of creating a family so  even though this language of donation is  similar across sperm donation and egg  donation what that donation is it looks  really different  how long do men typically stay sperm  donors yo